(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-hypotension-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Hypotension Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In vasovagal syncope, what finally causes the loss of consciousness?","choices":["Cerebral flow below autoregulation","Seizure activity across the cortex","Low blood glucose in the brain","A ventricular rhythm stopping flow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bradycardia, vasodilation and reduced output drop systolic pressure until cerebral blood flow falls below the limits of autoregulation and consciousness is lost. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How is reflex syncope diagnosed?","choices":["Mainly by serum electrolytes","Mainly by the history","Mainly by tilt-table testing","Mainly by echocardiography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. History is the most important portion of diagnosing reflex syncope, summed up as history, history, history, with a log of pattern and frequency. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which work-up is listed for arrhythmic cardiogenic syncope?","choices":["Electrocardiogram, monitor, stress test","Echocardiogram and stress echocardiogram","Emergent testing in the emergency room","Tilt-table test and an episode log"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Arrhythmia is investigated with an electrocardiogram, cardiac monitoring and a stress test, together with cardiology consultation. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Why does pressure fall on standing in neurogenic orthostatic hypotension?","choices":["Too little norepinephrine from sympathetic nerves","Excess vagal outflow triggered by fear","Reduced circulating volume from fluid loss","A fast ventricular rhythm reducing filling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Inadequate norepinephrine release from sympathetic postganglionic neurons means the vessels fail to constrict and the heart rate rises less than expected. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which are listed causes of non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension?","choices":["Hypovolemia, medication and advanced age","Diabetic, genetic and autoimmune disorders","Fear, pain and prolonged standing","Arrhythmia, infarction and embolism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Dehydration, blood loss, poor intake and medication use lower output or impair vasoconstriction without any primary autonomic disorder. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which examination finding supports diabetic autonomic neuropathy in a patient with orthostatic syncope?","choices":["A heart rate above 120 standing","Reduced sensation in both feet","Dry oral mucous membranes","A rapid, regular rhythm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Decreased vibration and light-touch sensation in both feet marks long-standing diabetes with peripheral neuropathy, which supports diabetic autonomic neuropathy as the cause. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which factors further predispose an older man to micturition syncope?","choices":["Prior infarction, low ejection fraction","Alcohol, dehydration, an alpha-1 blocker","Diabetes, Parkinson disease, neuropathy","Fear, pain and heat exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Alcohol use, relative dehydration and an alpha-1 blocker taken with an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor add to the risk of hypotension during nocturnal voiding. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which advice suits a patient with orthostatic syncope?","choices":["Restrict fluids each morning","Avoid all physical activity","Change position slowly","Stand up quickly to adapt"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Slow positional changes are listed treatment for orthostatic syncope, alongside treating the underlying cause, the real diagnosis. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the expected course of a syncopal episode?","choices":["Brief and self-limited","Prolonged coma needing support","Recovery only after glucose","Recovery with lasting confusion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Syncope is by definition transient and self-limited, followed by spontaneous recovery, which is part of what separates it from seizure. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"When the history cannot settle the source of orthostatic syncope, what is recommended?","choices":["Empiric vasopressors in the clinic","Reassurance with no further work-up","Repeat tilt-table tests until positive","Referral to the right specialty to confirm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. History is the most important tool for the diagnosis, or for referring to the right specialty for confirmation when the source is still unclear. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which feature characterizes chronic hypotension?","choices":["It ends once the trigger is removed","Its underlying cause may be unavoidable","It always has one specific trigger","It is sudden and always symptomatic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic hypotension waxes and wanes, is intermittently symptomatic, and its underlying cause, such as a cardiomyopathy, may not be removable. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which symptoms occur on standing in postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome?","choices":["Numb feet and dry mouth","Warmth and nausea while voiding","Palpitations and lightheadedness","Tongue biting and confusion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tachycardia with or without lightheadedness, dizziness, fainting and palpitations appears on standing and usually eases when lying down. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which cause of syncope is non-hypotensive?","choices":["Volume depletion","Vasovagal reflex","Autonomic failure","Hypoglycemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hypoglycemia is listed with seizure, toxic overdose, electrolyte imbalance, hypoxia, anxiety, anemia and the tachycardia syndrome as non-hypotensive causes. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which best describes a sign?","choices":["The named disease that explains it","A list of possible explanations","Objective, measurable evidence","A change the patient reports"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A sign is objective and measurable, such as a standing blood pressure; a symptom is a subjective change that the patient feels and reports. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which comorbidity is a reason to avoid midodrine?","choices":["Recent diarrheal illness","Chronic kidney disease","Long-standing diabetes","Ehlers-Danlos syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic kidney disease joins supine hypertension and severe heart disease as the settings in which midodrine is avoided. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which history points toward cardiogenic rather than reflex syncope?","choices":["Fainting at the sight of blood","Fainting after urinating at night","Abrupt collapse without any warning","Warmth, nausea and sweating beforehand"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Abrupt syncope with minimal prodrome, often after brief palpitations, in someone with coronary disease or reduced ejection fraction points to a cardiac cause. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which condition can progress to syncope if the pressure keeps falling?","choices":["Near-syncope","Postictal state","Supine hypertension","Relative bradycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Near-syncope is the dizziness, lightheadedness and tunnel vision that precede syncope, so it is the warning phase before consciousness is lost. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which referral applies to all three types of cardiogenic syncope?","choices":["Cardiology consultation","Neurology consultation","Endocrinology referral","Physical therapy referral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Arrhythmic, structural and vascular cardiogenic syncope each list cardiology consultation alongside their own tests. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is known about the cause of vasovagal syncope?","choices":["Structural heart disease","Unclear; possibly genetic","Autonomic nerve damage","Chronic volume depletion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The etiology of vasovagal syncope is unclear, with a possible genetic predisposition, even though its triggers and reflex chain are well described. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"With a sensitivity near 40% and specificity above 80%, what does a negative tilt-table test mean?","choices":["It reliably rules the syndrome out","It confirms autonomic failure","It points to a cardiac cause","It does little to exclude the syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Low sensitivity means many patients with the syndrome test negative, so a negative result tells almost nothing, while a positive result carries more weight. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why is patient education central to hypotension management?","choices":["It replaces the need to find a cause","It allows vasopressors to be stopped","It shortens the orthostatic test","Understanding is the key to compliance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A patient who understands why the hypotension happens is far more likely to follow slow position changes, trigger avoidance and medication changes. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which chain of events produces vasovagal syncope?","choices":["Sympathetic surge, tachycardia, constriction","Autonomic failure, fixed heart rate","Fluid loss, compensatory tachycardia","Vagal surge, bradycardia, vasodilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A transient rise in parasympathetic outflow with sympathetic inhibition causes bradycardia, vasodilation and reduced cardiac output, so systolic pressure falls. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What does symptomatic hypotension lead to?","choices":["Seizure from cortical irritation","Hypoglycemia from poor intake","Supine hypertension overnight","Syncope from cerebral hypoperfusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Syncope is the result of symptomatic hypotension: low peripheral resistance or low cardiac output lowers pressure until brain perfusion falls and consciousness is briefly lost. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which drug is preferred when supine hypertension limits therapy?","choices":["Phenylephrine","Norepinephrine","Droxidopa","Midodrine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Droxidopa has mild efficacy but is better with supine hypertension (high pressure while lying flat), the setting in which midodrine is avoided. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What does a significant heart rate rise during an orthostatic pressure fall indicate?","choices":["A primary cardiac arrhythmia","A reflex surge in vagal tone","An intact sympathetic reflex response","Autonomic failure from diabetes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A rise such as 68 to 92 per minute as pressure falls demonstrates an intact baroreceptor-mediated sympathetic response, which favors non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Why must a blood pressure reading be interpreted against the patient's age?","choices":["Age changes cuff accuracy only","Normal values change with age","Only adults can become hypotensive","Children tolerate any low pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blood pressure norms are affected by age, so a reading that is normal for an adult may be abnormal for an infant or child, and the reverse. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How is hypotension from infection classified by its time course?","choices":["Brief and self-limited","Intermittent and reflex","Long-lasting acute","Chronic and unavoidable"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Infection, including bacteremia, sepsis and septic shock, causes long-lasting acute hypotension, distinct from brief toxic causes and chronic cardiogenic ones. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"In neurogenic orthostatic hypotension, how is volume status usually described?","choices":["Volume status cannot be assessed","Volume depletion is usually absent","Volume depletion is often present","Volume overload is always present"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The problem is autonomic failure rather than fluid, so mucous membranes are typically moist and there is no evidence of dehydration. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"In which setting should midodrine be avoided?","choices":["Supine hypertension","Diabetic neuropathy","Parkinson disease","Multiple system atrophy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Midodrine is avoided with supine hypertension (high pressure while lying flat), severe heart disease and chronic kidney disease, despite being best with neurogenic syncope. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How is hypotension defined?","choices":["A systolic pressure under one fixed cutoff","Any faint that occurs on standing up","A pressure drop only when symptoms occur","A fall in systemic pressure below normal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hypotension is a decrease in systemic blood pressure below the normal level, and what counts as normal shifts with age and with the patient's own baseline and comorbidities. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 8"}]};
